### Challenges

Using CyberChef itself was not particularly difficult.

The difficulty was understanding the formats behind the operations.

For example, selecting `From Base64` takes seconds. Understanding why Base64 works requires thinking about ASCII values, binary representation, 6-bit groups, and the Base64 character table.

Likewise, URL decoding is easy to perform:

```text
%3A → :
%2F → /
%2E → .
```

But understanding why those values appear requires understanding percent-encoding and how characters are represented.

The same applies to Unix timestamps, Base85, Base58, Base62, binary, decimal, and the other formats available in CyberChef.

There is a substantial amount of knowledge hidden behind operations that appear to be very simple buttons.

### Mistakes

I did not run into any significant problems operating CyberChef itself.

Most of the slowdown came from stopping to understand what the different formats meant rather than simply applying the correct operation and moving on.

That distinction felt important.

Getting the expected output from a tool does not necessarily mean I understand what happened to the data.

### Lessons learned

* CyberChef provides a simple interface over many different data transformations.
* A recipe is a sequence of operations applied to an input.
* Operations can be chained together instead of performing each transformation manually.
* Base64 is an encoding, not encryption.
* Different base encodings represent binary data using different character sets.
* URL percent-encoding replaces certain characters with encoded representations.
* Unix timestamps represent time numerically relative to the Unix epoch.
* CyberChef can extract useful indicators such as IP addresses, URLs, and email addresses from larger datasets.
* Recognizing the format of the input is often more important than knowing where the button is.
* A transformation tool can perform the conversion for me, but I still need enough knowledge to understand whether the transformation makes sense.

### Reflection

CyberChef itself is surprisingly straightforward.

Most of the workflow is essentially:

> input → choose operation → build recipe → inspect output

What is considerably less straightforward is understanding the data.

There is a big difference between knowing:

> “Use `From Base64`.”

and understanding:

> “This data appears to be Base64, this is how that representation works, and decoding it is the appropriate transformation.”

That seems much more useful from a security perspective.

CyberChef removes much of the mechanical work involved in converting and inspecting data, but it does not remove the need to recognize what I am looking at.

In fact, the simplicity of the tool almost hides how much information sits underneath it.

Base64, Base85, Base58, Base62, hexadecimal, decimal, binary, URL encoding, Unix timestamps, character encodings, and other representations can each become their own subject.

So the lesson for me was not really how to use CyberChef.

The interface can be learned very quickly.

The real lesson was:

> The transformation may be easy. Recognizing and understanding the representation is the skill.

That also connects well with the previous Snort work. During packet investigation, I encountered things such as hexadecimal values, file signatures, HTTP payloads, and Base64-encoded commands.

CyberChef gives me a convenient way to investigate those representations without manually converting everything, while still requiring me to understand enough about the data to know what operation to apply.

### Next focus

